{
  "id": "ep160",
  "duration": 57.841,
  "beats": [
    {
      "start": 0.08,
      "end": 4.03,
      "text": "A tiny dwarf galaxy [bullied](/b\u02c8\u028ali\u02c8\u025bd/) a spiral twice our size.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Galaxy NGC 772 (noirlab2209a).tiff",
        "credit": "International Gemini Observatory/NOIRLab/NSF/AURA Image processing: T.A. Rector (University of Alaska Anchorage), J. Miller (Gemini Observatory/NSF's NOIRLab), M. Zamani & D. de Martin",
        "license": "CC BY 4.0",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Galaxy_NGC_772_(noirlab2209a).tiff"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 0.0,
          "end": 4.033,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 4.03,
      "end": 9.075,
      "text": "Our Milky Way spans a balanced pinwheel of sweeping, orderly spiral arms.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Artist\u2019s impression of the Milky Way.jpg",
        "credit": "NASA/JPL-Caltech/ESO/R. Hurt",
        "license": "Public domain",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Artist%E2%80%99s_impression_of_the_Milky_Way.jpg"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 4.033,
          "end": 5.9,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        },
        {
          "start": 5.9,
          "end": 9.067,
          "kind": "detail",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 9.075,
      "end": 18.77,
      "text": "Look across the constellation Aries, and unbarred spiral galaxy NGC 772 is significantly larger than our home galaxy.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Aries (Annotated) (aries-ann).tiff",
        "credit": "E. Slawik/NOIRLab/NSF/AURA/M. Zamani",
        "license": "CC BY 4.0",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aries_(Annotated)_(aries-ann).tiff"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 9.067,
          "end": 13.933,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        },
        {
          "start": 13.933,
          "end": 18.767,
          "kind": "detail",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 18.77,
      "end": 29.04,
      "text": "Yet its symmetry is shattered. NGC 772 is radically lopsided, dominated by one colossal runaway arm packed with dust lanes.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "NGC 772 (noao-n772block).tiff",
        "credit": "KPNO/NOIRLab/NSF/AURA/Adam Block",
        "license": "CC BY 4.0",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:NGC_772_(noao-n772block).tiff"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 18.767,
          "end": 29.033,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 29.04,
      "end": 35.385,
      "text": "The cosmic culprit is NGC 770, a nearby dwarf elliptical galaxy.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "NGC770 - SDSS DR14.jpg",
        "credit": "Sloan Digital Sky Survey",
        "license": "CC BY 4.0",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:NGC770_-_SDSS_DR14.jpg"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 29.033,
          "end": 33.367,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        },
        {
          "start": 33.367,
          "end": 35.4,
          "kind": "detail",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 35.385,
      "end": 42.48,
      "text": "Its relentless gravitational tides dragged and compressed the giant's disk, triggering furious stellar nurseries.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "NGC 772 A Rival to the Milky Way.jpg",
        "credit": "ESA/Hubble & NASA, A. Seth et al.",
        "license": "CC BY 4.0",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:NGC_772_A_Rival_to_the_Milky_Way.jpg"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 35.4,
          "end": 38.933,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        },
        {
          "start": 38.933,
          "end": 42.467,
          "kind": "detail",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 42.48,
      "end": 53.705,
      "text": "Star birth ran so violently that two separate Type II supernovae detonated in NGC 772 just seven weeks apart in 2003.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "GRB 020813- Cosmic Forensics Confirms Gamma-ray Burst-Supernova Connection (2003-grb020813 - grb020813 comp sized).tiff",
        "credit": "Illustration: CXC/M.Weiss; Spectrum: NASA/CXC/N.Butler et al.",
        "license": "Public domain",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:GRB_020813-_Cosmic_Forensics_Confirms_Gamma-ray_Burst-Supernova_Connection_(2003-grb020813_-_grb020813_comp_sized).tiff"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 42.467,
          "end": 48.067,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        },
        {
          "start": 48.067,
          "end": 53.7,
          "kind": "detail",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 53.705,
      "end": 57.491,
      "text": "Immense size could not protect this warped giant from an underdog.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Galaxy NGC 772 (noirlab2209a).tiff",
        "credit": "International Gemini Observatory/NOIRLab/NSF/AURA Image processing: T.A. Rector (University of Alaska Anchorage), J. Miller (Gemini Observatory/NSF's NOIRLab), M. Zamani & D. de Martin",
        "license": "CC BY 4.0",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Galaxy_NGC_772_(noirlab2209a).tiff"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 53.7,
          "end": 57.833,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
